{
  "reasoning": "The Sortino Ratio is a financial metric used to evaluate the risk-adjusted return of an investment, focusing specifically on downside risk. It is calculated using the formula: Sortino Ratio = (R_p - R_f) / σ_d, where R_p is the return of the portfolio (or investment), R_f is the risk-free rate, and σ_d is the standard deviation of the downside (losses). Given R_p = 14% (0.14), R_f = 4% (0.04), and σ_d = 7% (0.07), the calculation proceeds as follows: (0.14 - 0.04) / 0.07 = 0.10 / 0.07 ≈ 1.4286. Rounded to the nearest hundredth, the Sortino Ratio is 1.43.",
